# Catalog songs

Editorial recordings live in `src/lib/catalog/songs.ts` (not the MIDI
library). House style for lyrics and sourcing:
`.cursor/rules/history-articles.mdc`.

## Required on every new / expanded song

- Verified `youtubeId`, or `listen` to an archive when there is no legal
  player. Never invent an id.
- `genres` when an **existing** genre slug fits. Omit rather than invent.
- **Sourced `lyrics` on the song page.** Hunt first; copyright is not a
  skip. See “Lyrics — always hunt” below.
- **Non-English songs carry `original`** — the English name *and* the
  home-language name, both searchable. See “Bilingual titles” below.

## Lyrics — always hunt

Hearing a recording without the words is half a lesson. A new or
expanded song **must** get a sourced `lyrics` block, or a written reason
in `about` after a real hunt. Do **not** leave lyrics off because the
composition is still under copyright — that is how Over the Rainbow
shipped empty. Still-protected words are printed for teaching, with a
citable source, a link out, and the fair-use note `<SongLyrics>` already
shows.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — copyright used as a skip
about: "We do not print the English lyric here: it is still under copyright."

// ✅ GOOD — sourced, attributed, fair-use footer already on the page
lyrics: [{
  id: "english",
  label: "English — Harburg",
  lang: "en",
  language: "English",
  source: { label: "Leo Feist, Inc., 1939", url: "https://…" },
  lines: [/* transcribed from that publication, never from memory */],
}]
```

**Hunt order:** scholarly collections (Mlotek / Workers Circle for
Yiddish), Wikisource / IMSLP / LOC / university sheet-music scans for
public-domain songs, a cited encyclopedia or Wikipedia table that
actually prints the words, published sheet music we can point at.
Web-search; **never invent a line or “fix” one you did not see.**

**The only honest omissions** (say so in `about`):

1. **Instrumental** — no words exist (Dale’s Misirlou, Brandwein
   bulgars). Do not invent a lyric.
2. **Author forbade reprint** — e.g. Al Grand asked that his Yiddish
   Over the Rainbow not be used without permission. Hunt anyway; if the
   ask is explicit, omit and quote the request.
3. **Hunt failed** — no citable published text (not “I remembered it”).
   Write what you searched. A later pass must try again.

Non-English versions: original + line-by-line English gloss (`en`),
romanization (`latin`) when the script isn’t Latin. Several sung
versions → several `SongLyrics` entries. Don’t paste a lyric site with
no provenance (Genius, AZLyrics). Wikipedia / Wikisource / Milken /
Mlotek / LOC are fine when they print the words.

## Bilingual titles (non-English songs)

A song whose title is not English **must** name the tune in both languages,
and the global search bar (⌘K) **must** find it by either. English speakers
type “over the rainbow”; a Yiddish speaker types “iber dem regnboygn” or
`איבער דעם רעגן־בויגן`. Both land on the same song.

`title` stays the **best-known Latin display form** (unchanged for existing
rows — `Iber dem regnboygn`, `Bei Mir Bistu Shein`, `Misirlou`). The other
forms live in a structured `original` field:

```ts
/** Present iff the song’s title is not English. */
original?: {
  /** English title, or a literal gloss when there is no English title
   *  ("Over the Rainbow"; or "‘The calf’" for Dos kelbl). Required. */
  english: string;
  /** BCP-47 of the original language ("yi", "el", "ro"). Required. */
  lang: string;
  /** Romanization when the script isn’t Latin (YIVO for Yiddish, etc.).
   *  Usually equal to `title`; set it when `title` is a stylized variant. */
  latin?: string;
  /** Native-script spelling — **only when attested**. Never invent letters
   *  (YIVO for Yiddish, unpointed Hebrew, monotonic Greek). Omit if unsure. */
  native?: string;
};
```

Honesty (same bar as loanwords, `.cursor/rules/pronunciation-audio.mdc`):
`native` is an attested spelling or nothing — cite it in a short comment,
never guess script. `english` is the real English title when one exists; a
plain-language translation in quotes otherwise. Don’t label a proper-noun
tune (Misirlou) with a fake “English title” — give its native script + a
glossed meaning.

**Display** (render, don’t hand-format per page): `songTitleParts(song)` in
`songs.ts` returns `{ display, english?, native?, lang? }`. Surfaces:

- **Song page header, `/songs` cards, search rows** — show both: `title`
  with `— “{english}”`, and the native script (via `<NativeScript>`,
  dir-aware) when present. Never put `dir="rtl"` on a box that also holds
  Latin — see the loanword RTL note.
- **`<SongLink>` in article prose** keeps the single `title` inline (prose
  stays readable); the panel it opens shows the full bilingual title.

**Search** (both surfaces, same change):

- Global (`src/lib/search/index.ts`) — fold `original.english`,
  `original.latin`, `original.native`, and the language name into the song
  entry, and treat `english` as a **title alias** so an English-title query
  ranks it like a title hit, not a body hit.
- Hub (`src/lib/catalog/search.ts` → `songHaystack`) — add the same fields.

English-titled songs (blues catalog, `Over the Rainbow`) leave `original`
unset — they’re already in one language.

```ts
// ✅ GOOD — Yiddish song, both names, attested native script
{
  slug: "iber-dem-regnboygn",
  title: "Iber dem regnboygn",
  original: {
    english: "Over the Rainbow",
    lang: "yi",
    native: "איבער דעם רעגן־בויגן", // Grand’s Forward spelling, 2003
  },
  // …
}
```

## If a Cousins article names this song

Both directions, same change:

1. The article `members` list includes this slug.
2. This song has `cousins: ["<article-slug>"]`.

The song page **must** show those cousin links at the **top** (title →
attribution → genre pills → `COUSIN_SONG_HEADING` / `cousinSongLinkLabel`
from `src/lib/cousins/registry.ts`). Copy standard:
`.cursor/rules/cousins.mdc`. Don’t bury the card under the players.
`getCousinsBySong` is the lookup — don’t hand-roll a second list.

```ts
// ❌ BAD — mentioned in /cousins/miserlou, no reverse, no pills on either side
slug: "misirlou-dale"

// ✅ GOOD
slug: "misirlou-dale",
cousins: ["miserlou"],
genres: ["rock"],
```

Name a person → catalog artist + `<ArtistLink>` on first mention.
Name a foreign word → `WORDS` + first-mention wrap (human clip or no
speaker). `.cursor/rules/loanword-speakers.mdc`.
Keep one slug per recording/composition; don’t fork a second copy.
